[[:db/retract 17592186154162 :answer-field/choices 17592186154164] [:db/retract 17592186154162 :answer-field/choices 17592186154165] {:db/id 17592186157253 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-24483f16-f633-5136-92ad-93ac714083a9" "question-0c3ce769-6962-5734-97b4-e22ea97e78e5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157013 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to construct a direct proof of the following statement:\n\n$5n+4$ *is odd if* $n$ *is odd.*\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\nLet $n$ be an $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{odd}}}$ integer. Then, $n=\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{2a+1}}$ for some integer $a.$\n\nSubstituting $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{n=2a+1}}$ into the expression $5n+4$, we can show that $5n+4=\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{2b+1}},$ where $b$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{an integer}}}.$\n\nTherefore, since $5n+4$ $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{is one more than}}}$ a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that $5n+4$ is $\\bbox[3pt,border:solid 1pt]{\\phantom{\\textrm{odd}}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn \\text{ is odd}\n\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad\n5n+4 \\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\n*Let $n$ be an $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{odd}}$ integer. Then, $n=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2a+1}$ for some integer $a.$*\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into our expression $5n+4$ and show it is *odd* by writing it as *one more than* a multiple of $2.$\n\n*Substituting $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}n=2a+1}$ into the expression $5n+4$, we can show that $5n+4=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2b+1},$ where $b$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{an integer}}.$*\n\nOnce we've shown this, the proof is complete, and we can state our conclusion.\n\n*Therefore, since $5n+4$ $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{is one more than}}$ a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that $5n+4$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{odd}}.$*" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157254 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-15ce914f-28d4-58cf-a9d9-4237499cad54"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157054 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n![](images/74/74c3f2e4e2896418d438fee5f612965fe64e2bd7ecd5d7f92a24a3b7594e7092.svg)\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nIf $n$ is odd, then $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into the expression $7n-1$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n-1 & =7(2a+1)-1 \\\\  & =14a+7-1 \\\\  & =14a+6 \\\\  & =2{{(7a+3)}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=7a+3$ (highlighted above), we have that $7n-1 = 2b,$ which shows that $7n-1$ is indeed a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b = 7a+3.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7n-1 & =2{{(7a+3)}_{⏟}}_{b} \\\\  & =2b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$\n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, $7n-1$ is even." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157255 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b2a01950-32bd-52d0-9e4d-1134c0e8e132" "question-777be974-f06f-56a1-8838-5bb4b1e9b194"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157014 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n![](images/02/022c08614f15f942c5bb86928913458821e40c16549e7d08c70140db1eae776f.svg)\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\nLet $n$ be an even integer. Then, $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression $15n+7$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15n+7 & =15(2a)+7 \\\\  & =30a+7 \\\\  & =(30a+6)+1 \\\\  & =2{{(15a+3)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b = 15a+3$ (highlighted above), we have that $15n+7 = 2b+1,$ which shows that $15n+7$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b = 15a+3.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15n+7 & =2{{(15a+3)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one larger than a multiple of $2.$ \n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, $15n+7$ is odd." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157256 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-594c61de-dccf-595b-8086-329e505979dc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157055 :question/problem "Prove that $n^2 -  2$ is odd if $n$ is odd." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n![](images/b3/b346c9920bcd64b50c80e44521c87dc10a503b09c76867f45a78854aee22ce4e.svg)\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nSince $n$ is odd, it can be expressed as $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into our expression $n^2 -  2$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nNow, writing $n^2 -  2$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-2 & =(2a+1)^{2}-2 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}+4a+1)-2 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}+4a-2)+1 \\\\  & =2{{(2{a}^{2}+2a-1)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=2a^2 + 2a  - 1$ (highlighted above), we have that $n^2 -  2 = 2b+1,$ which shows that $n^2 -  2$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=2a^2 + 2a  - 1.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-2 & =2{{(2{a}^{2}+2a-1)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ \n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, $n^2 -  2$ is odd." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-24483f16-f633-5136-92ad-93ac714083a9" :question/id #uuid "24483f16-f633-5136-92ad-93ac714083a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-184162" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to construct a direct proof of the following statement:\n\nIf $n$ is an even integer, then ${n}^{3}+2n$ is even.\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\nLet $n$ be an {{field-1}} integer. Then, $n=$ {{field-2}} for some integer $a.$\n\nSubstituting {{field-3}} into the expression ${n}^{3}+2n$, we can show that ${n}^{3}+2n=$ {{field-4}}, where $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, since ${n}^{3}+2n$ {{field-6}} a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that ${n}^{3}+2n$ is {{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,{n}^{3}+2n\\text{ is even}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\nLet $n$ be an $\\text{even}$ integer. Then, $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression ${n}^{3}+2n$ and show it is *even* by writing it as a multiple of $2.$\n\nSubstituting $n=2a$ into the expression ${n}^{3}+2n$, we can show that ${n}^{3}+2n=2b,$ where $b$ is $\\text{an integer}.$\n\nOnce we've shown this, the proof is complete, and we can state our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, since ${n}^{3}+2n$ $\\text{is}$ a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that ${n}^{3}+2n$ is $\\text{even}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3398e270-a22b-559f-95cc-bbc33ca7a435" "answer-field-9608b260-4b04-5479-9720-4e619f18eaa9" "answer-field-50b91077-7898-5c7a-b6fe-9a60d25bcf94" "answer-field-4af3b643-aee3-5959-a51d-1457edab27e1" "answer-field-4aa3d6ba-3ad8-5bd3-82d0-e96043cfae7f" "answer-field-6b567856-7310-53ba-9762-54979bf870b2" "answer-field-d4e868f2-0598-5c86-b923-0b1c47f5f4ae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3398e270-a22b-559f-95cc-bbc33ca7a435" :answer-field/id #uuid "3398e270-a22b-559f-95cc-bbc33ca7a435" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-bafcdaae-f685-5b3b-bc1c-1c18ef68c6d2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bafcdaae-f685-5b3b-bc1c-1c18ef68c6d2" "answer-5a5cf516-3ad4-57ab-839f-ecc6df9d5109"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bafcdaae-f685-5b3b-bc1c-1c18ef68c6d2" :answer/id #uuid "bafcdaae-f685-5b3b-bc1c-1c18ef68c6d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "even" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a5cf516-3ad4-57ab-839f-ecc6df9d5109" :answer/id #uuid "5a5cf516-3ad4-57ab-839f-ecc6df9d5109" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "odd" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9608b260-4b04-5479-9720-4e619f18eaa9" :answer-field/id #uuid "9608b260-4b04-5479-9720-4e619f18eaa9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-0477f398-d8f2-5377-ad14-76cb1f284c76" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0477f398-d8f2-5377-ad14-76cb1f284c76" "answer-90a9ea15-95eb-5884-9384-c6e418909ba3"] :db/ensure [:answer-fiel