nswer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4bcf5ca-cb74-5117-a0e3-d2dd96a85127" :answer/id #uuid "e4bcf5ca-cb74-5117-a0e3-d2dd96a85127" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2a+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-faaec5e5-bcd6-5297-8010-8cd3a4985f4a" :answer/id #uuid "faaec5e5-bcd6-5297-8010-8cd3a4985f4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2b" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbbdaa56-5e52-558f-9b62-349e0f0a3077" :answer/id #uuid "bbbdaa56-5e52-558f-9b62-349e0f0a3077" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2b+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5e85c85-a6e1-5327-9ca8-26e74cbf577a" :answer/id #uuid "b5e85c85-a6e1-5327-9ca8-26e74cbf577a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2b-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-984e6594-129d-5f16-ac1e-883b51e34aea" :answer-field/id #uuid "984e6594-129d-5f16-ac1e-883b51e34aea" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-ffeeb179-3a71-51f0-ba13-7c26059ccba2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-55325dcb-51a4-5e00-8dc9-b65deb238a98" "answer-ffeeb179-3a71-51f0-ba13-7c26059ccba2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55325dcb-51a4-5e00-8dc9-b65deb238a98" :answer/id #uuid "55325dcb-51a4-5e00-8dc9-b65deb238a98" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "even" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffeeb179-3a71-51f0-ba13-7c26059ccba2" :answer/id #uuid "ffeeb179-3a71-51f0-ba13-7c26059ccba2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "odd" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-594c61de-dccf-595b-8086-329e505979dc" :question/id #uuid "594c61de-dccf-595b-8086-329e505979dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-184180" :question/problem "Prove that ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ is odd if $n$ is even.\n\nSince $n$ is even, it can be expressed as $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2n-3 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}. Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}+2n-3={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ is {{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,{n}^{2}+2n-3\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\nSince $n$ is even, it can be expressed as $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2n-3 & =(2a)^{2}+2(2a)-3 \\\\  & =4{a}^{2}+4a-3 \\\\  & =(4{a}^{2}+4a-4)+1 \\\\  & =2{{(2{a}^{2}+2a-2)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=2{a}^{2}+2a-2$ (highlighted above), we have that ${n}^{2}+2n-3=2b+1,$ which shows that ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=2{a}^{2}+2a-2.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+2n-3 & =2{{(2{a}^{2}+2a-2)}_{⏟}}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one more than a multiple of $2.$ \n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, ${n}^{2}+2n-3$ is odd." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b67678ce-b685-5fcb-ba5e-a183897c8dbc" "answer-field-c7fe2247-8e01-55d5-9716-f4386c744531" "answer-field-1893140b-2c20-54a2-ab36-4cfd4931dda0" "answer-field-d5f21e9d-b7d5-5dc3-b7be-894e42bdde85" "answer-field-e103f0fa-dc26-5bdf-a01f-8707518326d3" "answer-field-07ac89ac-d480-5f09-9c89-2320aa157bdd" "answer-